    <title>Questions</title> 

   <exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
    Let <m>V</m> be the set of all real sequences
    <me>(x_1,x_2,x_3,\dots)</me>
    such that <m>x_n \ge 0</m> for all <m>n</m>.
    Use the usual definitions of addition and scalar multiplication of sequences.

    Determine whether <m>V</m> is a vector space over <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> Think about additive inverses. </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p> <m>V</m> is not a vector space. </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The zero sequence belongs to <m>V</m>, and <m>V</m> is closed under addition.
      However, if <m>\mathbf{x}\in V</m> and <m>\mathbf{x}\neq\mathbf{0}</m>,
      then <m>-\mathbf{x}</m> has negative entries and does not belong to <m>V</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Thus additive inverses do not exist for all vectors,
      so axiom (A4) fails and <m>V</m> is not a vector space.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise> 

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p> Let <m>V=\mathbb{R}^2</m>.
    Define addition as usual, but define scalar multiplication by
    <me>c \odot (x,y) = (cx, |c|y).</me>

    Determine whether <m>(V,+,\odot)</m> is a vector space.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> Check whether <m>(-1)\odot(( -1)\odot\mathbf{v})</m> behaves as expected. </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p> <m>V</m> is not a vector space. </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m>\mathbf{v}=(0,1)</m>.
      Then
      <m>(-1)\odot\mathbf{v}=(0,1)</m>,
      since <m>|-1|=1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      But
      <m>(-1)(-1)\odot\mathbf{v} = 1\odot\mathbf{v}=(0,1)</m>,
      while associativity of scalar multiplication requires
      <m>(-1)((-1)\odot\mathbf{v}) = (1)\odot\mathbf{v}</m>
      to equal <m>(-1\cdot-1)\odot\mathbf{v}</m> for all vectors.
    </p>
    <p>
      The scalar multiplication axiom (SM3) fails,
      so <m>V</m> is not a vector space.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p> Let <m>V</m> be the set of all real polynomials.
    Define addition as usual, but define scalar multiplication by
    <me>c \odot p(x) = cp(x) + (1-c).</me>

    Determine whether <m>(V,+,\odot)</m> is a vector space.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> Identify the additive identity and test scalar distributivity. </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p> <m>V</m> is not a vector space. </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The additive identity with respect to usual addition is the zero polynomial,
      but
      <m>c\odot 0 = 1-c</m>,
      which is not the zero polynomial unless <m>c=1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Thus scalar multiplication does not preserve the additive identity,
      violating axiom (SM4).
      Therefore <m>V</m> is not a vector space.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

   <exercise>
  <statement>
    <p> Let <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> be the vector space of all real polynomials of degree at most <m>2</m>.
    Let <m>W</m> be the subset of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> consisting of those polynomials <m>p</m>
    that satisfy the condition
    <me>p'(0) + p(1) = 0.</me>

    <ol>
      <li><p> What is the dimension of <m>W</m>? </p></li>
      <li><p> Find a basis for <m>W</m>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> Write a general polynomial <m>p(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</m>.
    Compute <m>p'(x)</m>, then evaluate <m>p'(0)</m> and <m>p(1)</m>.
    The given condition gives a linear equation relating <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p> The dimension of <m>W</m> is <m>2</m>.
    One possible basis for <m>W</m> is
    <me>\{x^2 - 1,\; x - 2\}.</me>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m>p(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</m> be an arbitrary element of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.
      Then <m>p'(x) = 2ax + b</m>, so
      <me>p'(0) = b.</me>
      Also,
      <me>p(1) = a + b + c.</me>
      The condition <m>p'(0) + p(1) = 0</m> gives
      <me>b + (a + b + c) = 0,</me>
      which simplifies to
      <me>a + 2b + c = 0.</me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Solving for <m>c</m>, we obtain
      <me>c = -a - 2b.</me>
      Therefore every polynomial in <m>W</m> has the form
      <me>
        p(x) = a(x^2 - 1) + b(x - 2).
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      This shows that <m>W</m> is spanned by the polynomials
      <m>x^2 - 1</m> and <m>x - 2</m>.
      Since these polynomials are linearly independent,
      they form a basis for <m>W</m>.
      Hence
      <me>\dim W = 2.</me>
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise> 

 <exercise>
  <statement>
    <p> Let <m>V</m> be the vector space of all real sequences
    <me>(x_1, x_2, x_3, \dots)</me>

    with addition and scalar multiplication defined componentwise.</p>

    <p>
    Define
    <me>
      W = \{ \mathbf{x} \in V : x_{n+1} - x_n \text{ is constant for all } n \ge 1 \}.
    </me>

    <ol>
      <li><p> Show that <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>V</m>. </p></li>
      <li><p> Describe all sequences in <m>W</m> explicitly. </p></li>
      <li><p> Find a spanning set for <m>W</m> and determine the dimension of <m>W</m>. </p></li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> If the difference <m>x_{n+1} - x_n</m> is constant, try rewriting
    <m>x_{n+1}</m> in terms of <m>x_n</m>.
    Think about how many initial values determine an entire sequence in <m>W</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
    The set <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>V</m>.
    Every sequence in <m>W</m> has the form
    <me>x_n = a + (n-1)c</me>
    for real numbers <m>a</m> and <m>c</m>.
    A spanning set for <m>W</m> is
    <me>\{(1,1,1,\dots),(0,1,2,3,\dots)\}</me>
    and <m>\dim W = 2</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The zero sequence <m>(0,0,0,\dots)</m> satisfies
      <m>0 - 0 = 0</m> for all <m>n</m>, so <m>\mathbf{0} \in W</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y} \in W</m>.
      Then there exist constants <m>c</m> and <m>d</m> such that
      <m>x_{n+1} - x_n = c</m> and <m>y_{n+1} - y_n = d</m> for all <m>n</m>.
      Then
      <me>
        (x+y)_{n+1} - (x+y)_n
        = (x_{n+1}-x_n) + (y_{n+1}-y_n)
        = c + d,
      </me>
      which is constant. Hence <m>\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{y} \in W</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For any scalar <m>\alpha</m>,
      <me>
        (\alpha x)_{n+1} - (\alpha x)_n
        = \alpha(x_{n+1} - x_n)
        = \alpha c,
      </me>
      which is constant. Thus <m>\alpha \mathbf{x} \in W</m>.
      Therefore, <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>V</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If <m>\mathbf{x} \in W</m>, then there exists a constant <m>c</m> such that
      <m>x_{n+1} = x_n + c</m> for all <m>n</m>.
      Since we also have <m>x_{n}=x_{n-1} + c</m>, we also know that <md><mrow> x_{n+1} \amp = (x_{n-1} + c)+c </mrow> <mrow> \amp =x_{n-1}+2c.</mrow> </md>  Likewise, we have that <me>x_{n+1}=x_{n-2}+3c</me> and we conclude the pattern with 
      <me>x_n = x_1 + (n-1)c.</me>
      Hence every sequence in <m>W</m> is an <em>arithmetic</em> sequence.
    </p>

    <p>
      Any <m>\mathbf{x} \in W</m> can be written as
      <me>
        \mathbf{x}
        = x_1(1,1,1,\dots) + c(0,1,2,3,\dots).
      </me>
      Therefore,
      <me>
        W = \operatorname{span}\{(1,1,1,\dots),(0,1,2,3,\dots)\}.
      </me>
      These two sequences are linearly independent, so <m>\dim W = 2</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p> Let <m>V = M_{2,2}(\mathbb{R})</m> and define operations
    <m>\oplus</m> and <m>\odot</m> on <m>V</m> by
    <md>
      <mrow> A \oplus B \amp = A + B - I, </mrow>
      
      <mrow> c \odot A \amp = cA + (1-c)I.</mrow>
    </md>

    Show that <m>(V,\oplus,\odot)</m> is a vector space over <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
    Your justification should address the vector space axioms.
  </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> First identify the additive identity with respect to <m>\oplus</m>.
    Then determine the additive inverse of a general matrix <m>A</m>.
    Finally, check carefully how scalar multiplication interacts with addition.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p> Yes, <m>(V,\oplus,\odot)</m> is a vector space.
    The additive identity is <m>I</m>, the additive inverse of <m>A</m> is <m>2I-A</m>,
    and all vector space axioms are satisfied.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We verify the vector space axioms.
      Since <m>M_{2,2}(\mathbb{R})</m> is closed under ordinary matrix addition and scalar
      multiplication, and since <m>I \in V</m>, the set <m>V</m> is closed under
      <m>\oplus</m> and <m>\odot</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      To find the additive identity, suppose <m>Z \in V</m> satisfies
      <m>A \oplus Z = A</m> for all <m>A \in V</m>.
      Then
      <me>A + Z - I = A,</me>
      which implies <m>Z = I</m>.
      Thus the additive identity with respect to <m>\oplus</m> is <m>I</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>A \in V</m>.
      To find the additive inverse of <m>A</m>, we seek <m>B \in V</m> such that
      <m>A \oplus B = I</m>.
      This gives
      <me>A + B - I = I,</me>
      so <me>B = 2I - A.</me>
      Hence every element of <m>V</m> has an additive inverse.
    </p>

    <p>
      The operation <m>\oplus</m> is commutative and associative because ordinary matrix
      addition is commutative and associative, and the same matrix <m>I</m> is subtracted
      in every sum.
    </p>

    <p>
      We now verify compatibility of scalar multiplication.
      Let <m>c,d \in \mathbb{R}</m> and <m>A \in V</m>.
      Then
      <md>
        <mrow> c \odot (d \odot A)
        \amp = c \odot (dA + (1-d)I) </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = cdA + c(1-d)I + (1-c)I </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = (cd)A + (1-cd)I </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = (cd) \odot A. </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      We also check distributivity over addition.
      For <m>A,B \in V</m>,
      <md>
        <mrow> c \odot (A \oplus B)
        \amp = c \odot (A + B - I) </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = cA + cB - cI + (1-c)I </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = cA + cB +I - 2cI, </mrow>
      </md>
      while
      <md>
        <mrow> (c \odot A) \oplus (c \odot B)
        \amp = (cA + (1-c)I) + (cB + (1-c)I) - I </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = cA + cB +I - 2cI. </mrow>
      </md>
      Thus scalar multiplication distributes over addition.
    </p>

    <p>
      Finally,
      <me>1 \odot A = 1A + (1-1)I = A,</me>
      so the scalar identity axiom holds.
      Therefore all vector space axioms are satisfied, and
      <m>(V,\oplus,\odot)</m> is a vector space.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p> Let <m>V</m> be a vector space and let
    <m>S=\{\mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2,\mathbf{v}_3,\mathbf{v}_4\}\subseteq V</m>.
    Suppose that
    <me>\mathbf{v}_4=\mathbf{v}_1+\mathbf{v}_2</me>
    and that the set <m>\{\mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2,\mathbf{v}_3\}</m>
    is linearly independent.

    Find a basis for <m>\operatorname{span}(S)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> Decide whether <m>\mathbf{v}_4</m> adds anything new to the span. </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p> A basis for <m>\operatorname{span}(S)</m> is
    <m>\{\mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2,\mathbf{v}_3\}</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Since <m>\mathbf{v}_4</m> is a linear combination of
      <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>,
      it does not add anything new to the span.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because <m>\{\mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2,\mathbf{v}_3\}</m>
      is already linearly independent and spans the same space,
      it is a basis for <m>\operatorname{span}(S)</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p> Let <m>V=\mathbb{R}^n</m>, and let <m>A</m> be a fixed <m>n\times n</m> real matrix.
    Define
    <me>
      S = \{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^n : A\mathbf{x} = 2\mathbf{x}\}.
    </me>

    Determine whether <m>S</m> is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>.
    Justify your answer.
  </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> Suppose <m>\mathbf{x}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{x}_2</m> satisfy
    <m>A\mathbf{x}=2\mathbf{x}</m>.
    Check whether their sum and scalar multiples also satisfy this condition.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p> Yes, <m>S</m> is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m>\mathbf{x}_1,\mathbf{x}_2\in S</m>.
      Then
      <m>A\mathbf{x}_1=2\mathbf{x}_1</m>
      and
      <m>A\mathbf{x}_2=2\mathbf{x}_2</m>.
      Using linearity of matrix multiplication,
      <me>
        A(\mathbf{x}_1+\mathbf{x}_2)
        = A\mathbf{x}_1 + A\mathbf{x}_2
        = 2\mathbf{x}_1 + 2\mathbf{x}_2
        = 2(\mathbf{x}_1+\mathbf{x}_2).
      </me>
      Thus <m>\mathbf{x}_1+\mathbf{x}_2\in S</m>, so <m>S</m> is closed under addition.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>c\in\mathbb{R}</m> and let <m>\mathbf{x}_1\in S</m>.
      Then
      <me>
        A(c\mathbf{x}_1)
        = cA\mathbf{x}_1
        = c(2\mathbf{x}_1)
        = 2(c\mathbf{x}_1),
      </me>
      so <m>c\mathbf{x}_1\in S</m>.
      Thus <m>S</m> is closed under scalar multiplication.
    </p>

    <p>
      Since <m>A\mathbf{0}=2\mathbf{0}</m>, the zero vector belongs to <m>S</m>.
      Therefore <m>S</m> is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Note that the vectors in <m>S</m> are eigenvectors of <m>A</m> with eigenvalue <m>2</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p> Let <m>V = F(-\infty,\infty)</m> be the vector space of all real‑valued functions
    defined on <m>(-\infty,\infty)</m>.
    Define
    <me>
      S = \{ f \in V : f(x) = a\cos x + b\sin x + c
      \text{ for some } a,b,c \in \mathbb{R} \}.
    </me>

    Determine whether <m>S</m> is a subspace of <m>V</m>.
    Justify your answer.
  </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> Write two generic functions in <m>S</m> and compute their sum.
    Then check what happens when you multiply such a function by a scalar.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p> Yes, <m>S</m> is a subspace of <m>V</m>. </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be functions in <m>S</m>.
      Then there exist real numbers <m>a_1,b_1,c_1</m> and <m>a_2,b_2,c_2</m> such that
      <me>
        f(x)=a_1\cos x + b_1\sin x + c_1
        \quad \text{and} \quad
        g(x)=a_2\cos x + b_2\sin x + c_2.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Their sum is
      <me>
        f(x)+g(x)
        = (a_1+a_2)\cos x + (b_1+b_2)\sin x + (c_1+c_2),
      </me>
      which is again of the same form.
      Thus <m>S</m> is closed under addition.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>r \in \mathbb{R}</m>.
      Then
      <me>
        r f(x)
        = (r a_1)\cos x + (r b_1)\sin x + (r c_1),
      </me>
      which is also of the required form.
      Hence <m>S</m> is closed under scalar multiplication.
    </p>

    <p>
      The zero function belongs to <m>S</m> by taking
      <m>a=b=c=0</m>.
      Therefore <m>S</m> contains the zero vector.
    </p>

    <p>
      Since <m>S</m> contains the zero function and is closed under addition
      and scalar multiplication, it is a subspace of <m>V</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the dimension of <m>\mathcal{Z}_2</m>, the vector space of all
      <m>2\times 2</m> real matrices with zero trace.  Note: The trace is defined as the sum of the diagonal elements of a matrix.  
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Write a general <m>2\times 2</m> matrix, impose the zero‑trace condition,
      and count how many free parameters remain.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The dimension is <m>3</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      A general <m>2\times 2</m> real matrix has the form
      <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      The trace is <m>a+d</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The condition that the trace is zero gives
      <m>a+d=0</m>, or <m>d=-a</m>.
      Thus every matrix in <m>\mathcal{Z}_2</m> can be written as
      <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp -a \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The entries <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> are free,
      so the space depends on three independent real parameters.
      Therefore
      <m>\dim(\mathcal{Z}_2)=3</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>\mathcal{U}</m> be the set of all <m>2\times2</m> real matrices of the form
      <me>
        \begin{bmatrix}
          u \amp -u-x \\
          0 \amp x
        \end{bmatrix},
      </me>
      where <m>u,x \in \mathbb{R}</m>, and let <m>\mathcal{V}</m> be the set of all
      <m>2\times2</m> real matrices of the form
      <me>
        \begin{bmatrix}
          v \amp 0 \\
          w \amp -v
        \end{bmatrix},
      </me>
      where <m>v,w \in \mathbb{R}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Find a basis for <m>\mathcal{U}</m> and <m>\mathcal{V}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Write a general element of each set as a linear combination of matrices
      involving one parameter at a time.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      A basis for <m>\mathcal{U}</m> is
      <me>\left\{
      \begin{bmatrix}1\amp -1\\0\amp 0\end{bmatrix},
      \begin{bmatrix}0\amp -1\\0\amp 1\end{bmatrix}
      \right\}.</me>
      A basis for <m>\mathcal{V}</m> is
      <me>\left\{
      \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 0\\0\amp -1\end{bmatrix},
      \begin{bmatrix}0\amp 0\\1\amp 0\end{bmatrix}
      \right\}.</me>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Any matrix in <m>\mathcal{U}</m> can be written as
      <me>
        u\begin{bmatrix}1\amp -1\\0\amp 0\end{bmatrix}
        + x\begin{bmatrix}0\amp -1\\0\amp 1\end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
      These two matrices are linearly independent, so they form a basis for
      <m>\mathcal{U}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Any matrix in <m>\mathcal{V}</m> can be written as
      <me>
        v\begin{bmatrix}1\amp 0\\0\amp -1\end{bmatrix}
        + w\begin{bmatrix}0\amp 0\\1\amp 0\end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
      These matrices are linearly independent, so they form a basis for
      <m>\mathcal{V}</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>


<exercise label="choices-linind-dim">
  <title>Linear Independence and Dimension</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>V</m> be a vector space with <m>\dim V = 12</m>.
      Let <m>S</m> be a subset of <m>V</m> that is linearly independent and has
      <m>11</m> vectors.
      Which of the following statements is <em>false</em>?
    </p>
  </statement>

  <choices randomize="yes">
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        There must exist a linearly independent subset <m>S_1</m> of <m>V</m>
        such that <m>S \subseteq S_1</m> and <m>S_1</m> is not a basis for <m>V</m>.
      </statement>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        Every nonempty subset <m>S_1</m> of <m>S</m> is linearly independent.
      </statement>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        There must exist a linearly dependent subset <m>S_1</m> of <m>V</m>
        such that <m>S \subseteq S_1</m>.
      </statement>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        <m>\dim(\operatorname{span}(S)) &lt; \dim V</m>.
      </statement>
    </choice>
  </choices>
</exercise>

<exercise label="choices-cayley-hamilton">
  <title>Linear Dependence of Matrix Powers</title> 

  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A</m> be a <m>5\times5</m> matrix with real entries and let
      <m>\mathbf{x}\neq\mathbf{0}</m>.
      Consider the vectors
      <me>\mathbf{x},\; A\mathbf{x},\; A^2\mathbf{x},\; A^3\mathbf{x},\; A^4\mathbf{x},\; A^5\mathbf{x}.</me>
      Which of the following statements is correct?
    </p>
  </statement>

  <choices randomize="yes">
    <choice>
      <statement>
        The vectors are linearly independent.
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p> A vector space of dimension <m>5</m> cannot contain six linearly independent
        vectors. </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        The vectors are linearly dependent.
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p> A nontrivial linear combination of
        <m>\mathbf{x},A\mathbf{x},\dots,A^5\mathbf{x}</m> equal to <m>\mathbf{0}</m>. </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        The vectors are linearly independent if and only if <m>A</m> is symmetric.
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p> Symmetry of <m>A</m> has no bearing on linear dependence of these vectors. </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        Linear dependence or independence cannot be determined from the given data.
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p> Linear dependence occurs regardless of the choice of
        <m>A</m> and <m>\mathbf{x}</m>. </p>
        </feedback>
    </choice>
  </choices>
</exercise>
``

</exercises>

</section>
</handout>